            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your 1988 house has a few areas of air leakage heat loss that should be addressed:



** Some of the windows are not sealing well however you are planning to upgrade them anyway !



** The 2 x older bathroom exhaust fans



** There header space in the basement where the Central AC lines pass through to the outside.



** Both attic hatches (these can be sealed with a removable caulking, such as Draft Attack)



Savings: I modeled a modest 10% reduction in overall air leakage, that could potentially save $ 51 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.0 GJ, and qualify for $ 715 in rebates through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative. 



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $715 and $1300 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <ceilingsCathedral />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows>

You mentioned that you are considering an upgrade to most of the windows (excluding the 2 newer one in the rear main floor living room) and an upgrade to the front entry door system (including windows in doors &amp; sidelights), but not the newer garden doors in the back main floor living room. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading the older 13 windows with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged or fixed-frame windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/mÂ²K.



Savings:

By replacing all of the above windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 285 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 9.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 2,112.50 in rebates (13 windows @ $162.50 each) through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative. If you purchase windows with that have a U-Value of no higher than 1.05 W/mÂ²K, the rebate per window doubles to $325.00.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $162.50 or $325.00 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</windows>
                <doors>

You mentioned that you are considering an upgrade to most of the windows (excluding the 2 newer one in the rear main floor living room) and an upgrade to the front entry door system (including windows in doors &amp; sidelights), but not the newer garden doors in the back main floor living room. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading the front entry door system to a new ENERGY STARÂ® unit that has a U-Value no higher than 1.22 W/mÂ²K, or an Energy Rating no less than 34.



Savings:

By replacing this front entry door system with one that meets the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 4 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 0.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $162.50 in rebates through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative.



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $162.50 per hinged door system (includes all windows in the door, sidelights and transom windows above) is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</doors>
                <VentilationSystem>

Your house was initially designed to be quite air tight and required a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) unit to manage fresh air supply &amp; indoor air quality. The current VanEE unit appears to be original to the house, so it is now 30+ years old and likely beyond its' operational lifetime. It did not appear to be operating at the time of my visit to your house.



These older units usually have a heat exchange efficiency around 55% (at best) and they use alot of electricity. The new ENERGY STARÂ® HRV's being installed today operate at much higher efficiency (75% - 85%) and use ECM motor technology to cut the electricity use by about 75%. I recommend you upgrade to one of these newer ENERGY STARÂ® HRV's.



Savings:

By upgrading to a new ENERGY STARÂ® HRV that is a minimum 75% efficient and uses ECM motor technology, you could potentially save $ 69 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 1.8 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for HRV's from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem />
                <HeatingSystem />
                <Renewables>

Renewable Energy Systems



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a solar photovoltaic system technology that converts sunlight directly into electricity. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Batteries Connected to Photovoltaic Systems to Provide Standby Power for Home



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1300 is available for the installation of battery storage and an inverter to connect to a photovoltaic system to provide standby power for the home for an existing PV system or a new install. This resiliency measure must be combined with an energy efficiency retrofit from the Canada Greener Homes Grant in order to be eligible. This grant may be combined with the new PV system installation grant for a maximum grant of $5000. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.



Savings: As a starting point for your further investigation, I modeled a 3 kW PV system, flush mounted on the rear facing roof (Southwest), directly connected to the grid, without a battery-based standby power system. This is estimated to produce about 2,601 kWh annually, reducing your electricity bill by $ 874, and qualify for $3,900 from the Canada Greener Homes program.



</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.



Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources. Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) and the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Hay River, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment that vents to the outside (for your house, the jenn-aire downdraft vent, 2 bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 10.5 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment can be expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current wood stove draws its' combustion air from inside the house &amp; can back-draft combustion products into the house (especially when you open the door to place more wood on the fire).



During my assessment of your home, I noticed smoke detectors, however did not see any carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - An older HRV that did not appear to be operating

        - The jenn-aire downdraft vent for the kitchen range

        - 2 bathroom fans (operated by the bathroom light switch)

        - The clothes dryer



The older HRV should be upgraded to a new ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units (outlined previously in my report). The 2 bathroom fans are older and allow a fair bit of air leakage - they should be replaced with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units. For the new bathroom fans, you may want to consider splitting them away from the main light switch &amp; use a bathroom fan timer switch (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Nov. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $19.66 min, $0.33 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.37 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $0.80 per litre 

Propane - $0.66 per litre delivered, $2.46 per m3 piped to the house

Wood - $315.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.20 per bag, $268.12 per tonne bulk delivered



